{"file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/finite_spectral_shadows.tex","new_string":"\\paragraph{The adjoint-side kernel and the threshold audit.} The\nbookkeeping is run on the adjoint-side kernel $G_m=T^m(T^m)^*$, whose\nentries carry \\emph{complete source sums}: for states $\\xi=(x,\\sigma)$,\n$\\xi'=(x',\\sigma')$,\n\\[\nG_m(\\xi,\\xi')\n=\\sum_{\\substack{w\\to x,\\;w'\\to x'\\\\ B_m(w)-B_m(w')\\equiv\\sigma-\\sigma'\\ (2)}}\np_s(w)\\,p_s(w')\\;\\chi(2)^{\\sum_i(B_i'-B_i)}\n\\sum_{a\\in U_{3^c}}\\chi\\!\\Bigl(\\,\\prod_{i=1}^{m-1}\n\\frac{3^ia+C_i(w)}{3^ia+C_i(w')}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nso the coset dichotomy applies \\emph{pair by pair}: class I (nonstationary\nnondegenerate pairs) contributes exactly zero to every entry. In a full word\nenumeration at $c=4,5$ ($m=c$, sup row) all $5.6\\times10^5$ resp.\\\n$8.5\\times10^6$ class-I pairs give machine zeros, and the four-class\nabsolute mass reproduces the matrix row sum from above with $\\approx20\\%$\ninternal cancellation margin. The $G$-form row rates at $m=c$ are\n\\[\nR_G^{1/(2c)}=1.0743,\\;1.0294,\\;1.0003,\\;0.9854,\\;0.9720\n\\qquad(c=4,\\dots,8),\n\\]\ndescending and below $1$ from $c=7$. The exact class decomposition\nidentifies the controlling class: stationarity at threshold is the\n\\emph{congruence cascade} $v_3(\\Delta C_i)\\ge c-1-i$ for every interior $i$,\nequivalently the nested conditions $B_i\\equiv B_i'\\pmod{2\\cdot3^{\\,c-2-i}}$,\none factor of $3$ deeper per level of $c$. The twisted near-locked pairs\nsatisfying this cascade (class II) carry $58\\%$ resp.\\ $48\\%$ of the sup-row\nmass at $c=4,5$ with per-class rate $1.0373\\to0.9762$ (below $1$ from\n$c=5$); the locked class III is second ($0.9883\\to0.9618$); the degenerate\nclass IV is subordinate ($0.8406$); the untwisted remainder is negligible\n($0.60$). The remaining finite-side statement is therefore:\n\n\\begin{conjecture}[Threshold Schur theorem; $m=c$]\\label{conj:schur}\nFix $s=0$ and the unit-mass normalization $T=W_s^{-1}M_{\\chi,c,s}$. There\nexist $\\delta>0$ and $c_0$ such that for all $c\\ge c_0$, uniformly over\nprimitive non-coboundary $\\chi$ modulo $3^c$,\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\xi}\\sum_{\\xi'}\\bigl|G_c(\\xi,\\xi')\\bigr|\\le(1-\\delta)^{2c},\n\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\n\\rho(T_{\\chi,c,0})\\le\\lVert T_{\\chi,c,0}^{\\,c}\\rVert^{1/c}\\le1-\\delta.\n\\]\nThe proof program is a four-budget row-sum decomposition: $R_{\\mathrm I}=0$\n(proved, exact vanishing); $R_{\\mathrm{III}}$ explicit (locked-prefix\ncollision mass $\\Delta_0^{\\,c-1}$ times damped last-symbol multiplicity);\n$R_{\\mathrm{IV}}$ subordinate by the degenerate-minimum audit (mass\ntreatment valid precisely at $m=c$); and the one missing lemma, an\n\\emph{endpoint-thin (near-locked cascade) bound}: the weighted mass of word\npairs satisfying the congruence cascade with $2^{\\Delta B}\\ne1$ decays\ngeometrically in $c$---a nested-congruence counting problem of the same\nrenewal-rigidity type as the proved flattening lemma (\\cref{lem:flat}). The\n$m=2c$ singular-value decay (rate $\\approx0.70$) is real but not presently\naccessible to absolute-value bookkeeping; the threshold $m=c$ is the regime\nwhere completeness first appears while endpoint matching still suppresses\ndegenerate nonstationary pairs. Empirical scale: $1-\\delta\\approx0.97$ at\n$c=8$, descending. The uniform-$s$ extension is a subsequent target. This is\na fixed-power operator-norm statement---outside the scope of the trace\nceiling (\\cref{rem:sixth})---and it implies $\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1$ directly.\n\\end{conjecture}","old_string":"The remaining finite-side statement is therefore:\n\n\\begin{conjecture}[Large-$m$ complete-kernel Schur contraction]\\label{conj:schur}\nFix $s=0$ and the unit-mass normalization $T=W_s^{-1}M_{\\chi,c,s}$. There\nexist $\\alpha\\ge1$, $\\delta>0$ and $c_1$ such that for all $c\\ge c_1$, with\n$m=\\lfloor\\alpha c\\rfloor$ and uniformly over primitive non-coboundary $\\chi$\nmodulo $3^c$,\n\\[\n\\lVert T_{\\chi,c,0}^{\\,m}\\rVert_{2\\to2}\\le(1-\\delta)^m,\n\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad\nR_m\\le(1-\\delta)^{2m}.\n\\]\nBy the audit it suffices to prove $A_m(c)\\le C^m$ with $C<3$: the word-pair\nbookkeeping must beat a row-multiplicity growth rate of $3^m$ in the complete\nsums of \\cref{prop:completekernel}. By the degenerate-minimum audit the\nviable choice is $\\alpha=1$, with empirical scale $1-\\delta\\approx0.93$ at\n$m=c$ (the rate $\\approx0.70$ at $m=2c$ is not provable by mass bookkeeping\nalone); neither rate is part of the statement. The\nuniform-$s$ extension ($\\Delta_s$ in place of $\\Delta_0$, $s\\in[0,1]$) is a\nsubsequent target. This is a fixed-power operator-norm statement---outside\nthe scope of the trace ceiling (\\cref{rem:sixth})---and it implies\n$\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1$ directly. The numerical singular-value data do not yet\nprove the uniform gap; they identify the correct non-trace norm in which the\nalready-proved vanishing and flattening lemmas\n(\\cref{lem:vanish,lem:coset,lem:flat}) should be assembled, via a row-sum\ndecomposition of the word pairs into nonstationary, thin, prefix-locked\nstationary, and degenerate-minimum classes---the stationary directions being\nspectrally inert by \\cref{thm:eject}.\n\\end{conjecture}","replace_all":false}